{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-10-01 17:29:14 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-02 00:29:14 +0700\n\nRead the complete C2 findings and checked the relevant lowering, prelude, and collector code. Read-only; no edits or tests. I concur with all nine recommendations, subject to the conditions below.\n\n1. **CONCUR — Per-generator handler stack and boundary frame.** This preserves C1’s absolute handler snapshots without rebasing them. Every crossing must restore the receiving context before delivering a value or raising an exception; validation failures must leave the generator unchanged. Test nested generators, different resuming callers, and suspension during exception unwinding. **C3 interaction:** integer values in handlers, locals, and outcomes remain ordinary immutable scalar payloads; the generator alone supplies mutable identity.\n\n2. **CONCUR — Flag-cell pass discriminator.** Use the already-established C1 pattern and enforce one invocation per captured continuation. Do not inspect continuation representation. Strengthen the allocation claim: flags become collectible when unreachable, not necessarily immediately after re-entry as stated at [C2 findings:124](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790874900000-architect-python-c2-generators-design.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:124); captured environments can retain them. Measure reachable heap after repeated suspension and collection. **C3 interaction:** numeric promotion must not allocate additional task cells or change these GC allocation counts.\n\n3. **CONCUR — Single completion exit with tagged outcomes.** Preserve `[:yield v]`, `[:return v]`, and `[:raise e]` internally; generator protocol adapters translate completion into `StopIteration`, while `next(g, default)` consumes completion directly. Escaping body `StopIteration` receives PEP 479 conversion; delegated iterator termination must first be consumed by `yield from`, preserving `.value`. If `close()` encounters a yield, report `RuntimeError` while retaining the resulting suspended state—do not falsely mark it closed. **C3 interaction:** yielded, sent, and returned bignums retain exact value and encoding.\n\n4. **CONCUR — Remove consuming-builtin inlining in S4.** Lower generator expressions uniformly to lazy generator objects, including arguments to `sum`, `any`, and `all`. Test short-circuiting and side effects as well as PEP 479: eager materialization can execute expressions that a lazy consumer never reaches. Evaluate and validate the outermost iterable at creation; defer the remaining clauses. Any later optimization must preserve these observations and runtime rebinding.\n\n5. **CONCUR — Retain `py/iter-at`’s index signature.** Treat it as an internal compatibility interface, not the public iterator protocol. Stateful iterators advance once per call and ignore the supplied index; iterable acquisition occurs once per loop, and `iter(iterator)` preserves identity. Narrow exhaustion handling to the advancement operation, never the loop body. **C3 interaction:** the vestigial loop counter must not eventually overflow or round during long generator consumption; use exact increment once C3 lands, without narrowing generator payloads to index-sized integers.\n\n6. **CONCUR — Reclaim dropped generators without implicit `close()`, as an explicit support restriction.** Correct the claim of language-reference conformity at [C2 findings:121](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790874900000-architect-python-c2-generators-design.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:121): Python specifies a `close()` call upon generator finalization, so this is broader than merely declining CPython’s refcount timing. [Python generator finalization](https://docs.python.org/3.11/reference/expressions.html#yield-expressions). Preserve the landed no-finalizers collector; explicit `close()` remains supported. Pinning roots the generator cell and traces its **current** reachable content, not every historical continuation permanently ([engine.cljc:1983](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:1983), [engine.cljc:2039](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:2039)). **C3 interaction:** unreachable bignum payloads require no finalization or separate pinning.\n\n7. **CONCUR — Accept stale-base retention for C2, with a stronger bounded-retention gate.** Undelimited capture may retain the initial caller, but acceptance must exclude a growing chain of previous callers or obsolete generator states. Test the complete reachable continuation/heap graph across many yields, changing callers, nested delegation, and forced collections; top-level continuation length alone is insufficient. Clearing named slots does not prove equivalent references vanished from captured environments. **C3 interaction:** stale frames can retain large integers, so measure retained payload size as well as cell count. Generator migration remains refused; scalar bignum portability does not remove the cell or continuation barriers.\n\n8. **CONCUR — Include user-defined iterator classes in C2 S5.** This belongs with generators, not C3 numeric work. Strengthen “two prelude arms”: implicit `__iter__`/`__next__` lookup must use the type’s special-method path, validate the returned iterator, and catch `StopIteration` only around advancement. Ordinary `py/getattr` is insufficient because it prioritizes instance attributes ([prelude.cljc:504](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:504)). Preserve termination values for delegation and propagate other exceptions; forward optional `send`/`throw`/`close` according to the delegation protocol. [PEP 380](https://peps.python.org/pep-0380/). Test shared iterators, invalid `__iter__` results, subclassed `StopIteration`, and exceptions from loop bodies.\n\n9. **CONCUR — No persisted generator flag.** Determine generator status during scope-aware lowering, excluding nested function bodies while including unreachable `yield` and `yield from`; encode the resulting generator-producing behavior in ordinary code. The query described at [C2 findings:129](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790874900000-architect-python-c2-generators-design.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:129) needs strengthening: a `yield from`-only body need not directly apply `py/yield`. Future introspection should first derive status from the scoped generator-construction pattern, not automatically add a literal flag. **C3 interaction:** neither feature needs a new AST tag or generator-specific numeric encoding.\n\nAcross these rulings, require all four evaluators on JVM, Node, and Dart; “wherever the parity lane runs” is insufficient for a portability claim. Generator snapshot rendering remains diagnostic data, not a resumable wire representation. Generator key identity remains reference-based and separate from C3’s numeric equality and hash normalization.\n\n### Converged rulings\n\n1. Adopt per-generator handler contexts with a boundary frame; restore the receiving context before delivering every crossing outcome.\n2. Use flag cells to distinguish capture passes; invoke each captured continuation once and verify bounded reachable allocation.\n3. Route generator completion through tagged outcomes; translate at protocol boundaries and preserve PEP 479, delegation values, and failed-close suspension.\n4. Remove consuming-builtin generator-expression inlining; preserve lazy consumption, eager outermost iterable acquisition, and runtime rebinding.\n5. Retain `py/iter-at`’s signature internally; advance stateful iterators once per call and prevent counter overflow under C3.\n6. Reclaim unreachable generators without implicit close as an explicit finalization restriction; retain explicit close and existing current-reachability pinning.\n7. Accept fixed stale-base retention for C2; reject per-yield retention growth, defer delimited capture, and preserve generator migration refusals.\n8. Include user-defined iterators in C2 S5 with type-based special-method lookup, validated iterator results, and narrowly scoped exhaustion handling.\n9. Persist no generator flag; classify during scoped lowering and derive future introspection from generator-producing code structure."}}
